I have created a server component that displays a welcome message and logs the page view without blocking the response.

I used the Next.js `after` API from `next/server`, which is the recommended approach for scheduling secondary tasks (like logging or analytics) to run after the primary response has been successfully sent to the client.

You can find the component in `/vercel/sandbox/app/WelcomeComponent.tsx`:

```tsx
import { after } from 'next/server';

async function logAnalytics() {
  // Simulate an analytics service call
  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 500));
  console.log('Page view successfully logged to analytics service.');
}

export default function WelcomeComponent() {
  // Schedule the analytics logging to run after the response is sent to the client,
  // ensuring it does not block the initial page load.
  after(async () => {
    await logAnalytics();
  });

  return (
    <div>
      <h1>Welcome to our Application!</h1>
      <p>We are glad to have you here. Your visit has been noted without slowing down this page.</p>
    </div>
  );
}
```

This component is a React Server Component and is fully type-checked. You can now import and use `<WelcomeComponent />` in your `page.tsx` or any other server component.

